{"qid":"Q167639","names":["Vijayanagara Empire"],"title":"Vijayanagara Empire","endonym":{"native":"ವಿಜಯನಗರ ಸಾಮ್ರಾಜ್ಯ","lang":"kn"},"capsule":"The Vijayanagara Empire (ವಿಜಯನಗರ ಸಾಮ್ರಾಜ್ಯ) was founded in 1336 by Harihara I and Bukka I, brothers who established the capital Vijayanagara on the south bank of the Tungabhadra River in the Deccan, creating a major Hindu polity that resisted northward Bahmani Sultanate expansion. At its apex under Krishnadevaraya (r. 1509–1529), the empire controlled virtually the entire Indian peninsula south of the Krishna River, patronized Telugu and Kannada literature, and maintained diplomatic contact with the Portuguese at Goa. The empire was decisively broken at the Battle of Talikota in January 1565, when a coalition of Deccan Sultanates defeated the Vijayanagara army and sacked the capital; successor ruling houses in Penukonda and Chandragiri maintained diminished authority until the last attested rulers in the 1640s.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The core Vijayanagara territory is well attested through Telugu and Kannada inscriptions and Portuguese traveler accounts; exact northern boundaries fluctuated with Bahmani and successor Sultanate contests and are reconstructed from inscriptional surveys.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Vijayanagara Empire","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vijayanagara_Empire"},{"label":"Britannica: Vijayanagar empire","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Vijayanagar-empire"},{"label":"Stein, B., Vijayanagara (Cambridge University Press, 1989) — New Cambridge History of India II.1","url":"https://www.cambridge.org/core